  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_CHEMICALCOORDINATIONANDINTEGRATION_DIAGRAM_Q02 - Hypothalamus and pituitary regulatory pathways</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>The diagram below illustrates the anatomical and regulatory relationship between the hypothalamus and the two lobes of the pituitary gland, labeled <strong>X</strong> and <strong>Y</strong>. Which statement accurately describes the regulation of <strong>X</strong> and <strong>Y</strong>?</p>
      <p>
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 380 270" width="380" height="270">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="380" height="270" fill="#ffffff"/>
          <!-- Hypothalamus region -->
          <path d="M 60 30 Q 190 15 320 30 L 300 105 Q 190 115 80 105 Z" fill="#e2e8f0" stroke="#4a5568" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="145" y="45" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#2d3748">Hypothalamus</text>
          <!-- Neurosecretory neuron 1 (to portal system) -->
          <circle cx="135" cy="70" r="10" fill="#feb2b2" stroke="#9b2c2c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="135" y1="80" x2="135" y2="128" stroke="#9b2c2c" stroke-width="3"/>
          <!-- Neurosecretory neuron 2 (direct to posterior pituitary) -->
          <circle cx="245" cy="70" r="10" fill="#feb2b2" stroke="#9b2c2c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="245" y1="80" x2="245" y2="205" stroke="#9b2c2c" stroke-width="3"/>
          <!-- Portal circulation -->
          <ellipse cx="135" cy="145" rx="22" ry="12" fill="none" stroke="#dd6b20" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4,2"/>
          <line x1="135" y1="157" x2="135" y2="185" stroke="#dd6b20" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <text x="20" y="150" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#9c4221">Portal</text>
          <text x="15" y="164" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#9c4221">circulation</text>
          <!-- Anterior pituitary (X) -->
          <ellipse cx="135" cy="215" rx="45" ry="30" fill="#fbd38d" stroke="#9c4221" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="129" y="220" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#1a202c">X</text>
          <!-- Posterior pituitary (Y) -->
          <ellipse cx="245" cy="215" rx="40" ry="28" fill="#feebc8" stroke="#9c4221" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="239" y="220" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#1a202c">Y</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Layout and Given Conditions</strong><br/>The schematic depicts two hypothalamic neurosecretory pathways. The left pathway terminates on a portal circulatory system connected to lobe <strong>X</strong>, while the right pathway extends directly via axons into lobe <strong>Y</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>Hypothalamic hormones regulate the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) and posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) through distinct anatomical routes.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Evaluation of the Pathways</strong><br/>Releasing and inhibiting hormones originating in hypothalamic neurons pass through axons, are released at nerve endings, and reach the anterior pituitary (<strong>X</strong>) via a portal circulatory system to regulate its secretions (such as GH, PRL, TSH, ACTH, LH, and FSH). In contrast, the posterior pituitary (<strong>Y</strong>) is under the direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus, receiving oxytocin and vasopressin axonally for storage and release.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>Region <strong>X</strong> (anterior pituitary) is regulated by hypothalamic hormones transported through the portal circulatory system, whereas region <strong>Y</strong> (posterior pituitary) is under the direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>X</strong> is under direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus and stores oxytocin and vasopressin.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Y</strong> receives hypothalamic releasing hormones through the portal circulatory system to secrete GH and TSH.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>X</strong> is regulated by hypothalamic hormones via the portal circulatory system, whereas <strong>Y</strong> is under the direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>X</strong> is the pars intermedia which secretes melatonin, whereas <strong>Y</strong> is the pars distalis which secretes MSH.</p>]]></text></answer>
