  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_CHEMICALCOORDINATIONANDINTEGRATION_DIAGRAM_Q04 - Mechanisms of membrane-bound and nuclear receptor hormone action</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>The schematic below contrasts two cellular mechanisms of hormone action for hormones <strong>H<sub>1</sub></strong> and <strong>H<sub>2</sub></strong>. Which pair of hormones correctly corresponds to <strong>H<sub>1</sub></strong> and <strong>H<sub>2</sub></strong> respectively?</p>
      <p>
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 260" width="420" height="260">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="420" height="260" fill="#ffffff"/>
          <!-- Divider -->
          <line x1="210" y1="15" x2="210" y2="245" stroke="#cbd5e0" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5,5"/>
          <!-- Pathway 1 (Membrane-bound receptor) -->
          <text x="20" y="30" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#2b6cb0">H1</text>
          <circle cx="55" cy="26" r="9" fill="#2b6cb0"/>
          <line x1="65" y1="32" x2="92" y2="55" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- Cell membrane 1 -->
          <path d="M 20 65 Q 105 55 190 65" fill="none" stroke="#d69e2e" stroke-width="6"/>
          <rect x="92" y="54" width="22" height="18" fill="none" stroke="#c53030" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="122" y="52" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#4a5568">Membrane receptor</text>
          <line x1="103" y1="75" x2="103" y2="105" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="35" y="122" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#1a202c">Second messenger</text>
          <text x="52" y="137" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#4a5568">(cAMP / Ca²⁺)</text>
          <line x1="103" y1="145" x2="103" y2="175" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="42" y="192" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#1a202c">Biochemical responses</text>
          <line x1="103" y1="200" x2="103" y2="225" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="38" y="242" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#1a202c">Physiological responses</text>
          <!-- Pathway 2 (Intracellular/nuclear receptor) -->
          <text x="230" y="30" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#2f855a">H2</text>
          <circle cx="265" cy="26" r="9" fill="#2f855a"/>
          <!-- Cell membrane 2 -->
          <path d="M 230 65 Q 315 55 400 65" fill="none" stroke="#d69e2e" stroke-width="6"/>
          <!-- Nucleus -->
          <circle cx="315" cy="145" r="52" fill="#f0fff4" stroke="#2f855a" stroke-width="2"/>
          <line x1="272" y1="34" x2="305" y2="120" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <circle cx="310" cy="128" r="7" fill="#2f855a"/>
          <text x="272" y="150" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#1a202c">Hormone-receptor</text>
          <text x="292" y="162" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#1a202c">complex</text>
          <line x1="315" y1="168" x2="315" y2="184" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="260" y="196" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#1a202c">Genome → mRNA → Proteins</text>
          <line x1="315" y1="204" x2="315" y2="225" stroke="#1a202c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="250" y="242" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#1a202c">Physiological responses</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Layout and Given Conditions</strong><br/>The left panel depicts hormone \(\mathrm{H}_1\) binding to a receptor on the cell membrane without entering the cell, generating a second messenger (such as cyclic AMP or \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)) to produce biochemical and physiological responses. The right panel depicts hormone \(\mathrm{H}_2\) crossing the cell membrane and binding to an intracellular nuclear receptor to form a hormone-receptor complex that interacts with the genome to regulate mRNA and protein synthesis.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>Hormones are classified by their chemical nature and receptor location: peptide, polypeptide, protein hormones, and amino-acid derivatives (such as epinephrine) typically bind membrane-bound receptors and act via second messengers, whereas steroid hormones and iodothyronines enter target cells to bind intracellular (nuclear) receptors and regulate gene expression.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Evaluation of Hormone Types</strong><br/>Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) is a protein/pituitary hormone that interacts with membrane-bound receptors and generates second messengers like cyclic AMP or \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), matching pathway \(\mathrm{H}_1\). Estrogen is a steroid hormone that crosses the cell membrane and binds to nuclear receptors to regulate gene expression, matching pathway \(\mathrm{H}_2\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>\(\mathrm{H}_1\) corresponds to follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) and \(\mathrm{H}_2\) corresponds to estrogen.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
